# Every text file is checked out with LF, on every platform.
#
# This is not a formatting preference. Two files in this repository are read
# byte for byte and shipped somewhere else to be executed, rather than only
# being edited here:
#
#   infra/device.sh              rendered into an instance's user data by
#                                `templatefile` and run by cloud-init
#   infra/device-requirements.txt  embedded in that script's heredoc
#
# Git's `core.autocrlf` is true on Windows, which stores LF and checks out CRLF.
# A device provisioned from a Windows clone would therefore receive a script
# whose every line ends in a carriage return, starting with a shebang naming
# `/bin/bash\r` -- an interpreter that does not exist. First boot fails, the
# nucleus is never installed, and the only symptom is a device that never
# reports, which is the one canary outcome that says nothing about the model.
# The same apply from CI would succeed, because its runner checks out LF.
#
# Applied to the whole tree rather than to those two paths, because the failure
# is not a property of shell scripts -- it is a property of any file whose bytes
# leave this machine. The source archives the training job and the replay
# component receive are built from the working tree too, so a Windows checkout
# and a Linux one would otherwise pack different bytes for one commit.
#
# `text=auto` leaves detected binaries alone. Nothing committed changes: with
# `core.autocrlf` true these files are already stored with LF, so this decides
# checkout and nothing else.
* text=auto eol=lf
